# The ten tasks of the P1 protocol's THIRD round, fixed before the mechanism it
# will measure was built.
#
# WHY A THIRD SET. Round 2 measured the surface that answers round 1 and the
# answer was two-sided: the surface beat our own uncharged arm by 73.6 points,
# and it did not beat the memory the coding agent already ships (-8.3 points,
# `mnema+` 91.7% against `host` 100.0%). The whole of that gap is one task, where
# our arm scored 0.50 and the host's 1.00. Round 3 measures the answer to that
# one task. Rounds 1 and 2 spent twenty tasks between them -- the held-out ones
# were touched once by rule, the development ones were iterated against -- so
# proving anything new needs tasks nobody has spent. These ten are new, they
# share no id with either earlier round, and they were frozen BEFORE the
# mechanism this round exists to measure is written.
#
# AND THIS FREEZE IS WEAKER THAN ROUND 2'S, IN ONE NAMED WAY. Round 2's tasks
# were written by somebody who could not know what the mechanism would do,
# because it did not exist. These were written by somebody who knew round 2's
# result and had already formed a hypothesis about its one bad task. No hash
# fixes that. What was done about it is written in `prediction.md`, including the
# fact that the failing task's contents were not opened while these were being
# written, and that the prediction about them was declared rather than the tasks
# being selected. The reader is told the direction of the weakness because that
# is the only protection that does not lie.
#
# Each hash covers one task directory whole -- its starting repository, its
# ticket, its decision, its two references and its discriminant -- over a
# DETERMINISTIC archive: entry order fixed, timestamps and ownership zeroed,
# Python bytecode excluded. The archive carries the task's id as its path prefix,
# so two tasks cannot swap contents without both hashes moving.
#
# The tasks themselves are withheld until they have been used. Until then this
# file is what a third party can check: not what a task says, but that it did not
# change between the freeze and the result.
#
# REPRODUCE -- GNU tar 1.35 and coreutils sha256sum, run from the directory that
# holds the ten task directories:
#
#   for id in a15-commission-base a16-event-order a17-billed-seats \
#             a18-dunning-recipients a19-batch-pick a20-over-long-note \
#             a21-session-expiry a22-graduated-usage b5-column-name \
#             b6-wrap-words; do
#     printf '%s  %s\n' "$(tar --sort=name --format=gnu \
#         --mtime='UTC 1970-01-01' --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner \
#         --exclude='__pycache__' --exclude='*.pyc' \
#         -cf - "$id" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$id"
#   done
#
# A hash nobody knows how to reproduce proves nothing, which is why the command
# is here and not in a report. A different tar implementation will produce a
# different stream: the version above is the one these hashes were taken with,
# and the harness's own `digestOf` was run against these ten and agreed with it
# hash for hash.
#
